      "content": "Civil liberties advocates often argue from foundational legal principles, asserting that the Fourteenth Amendment's protection of bodily autonomy prevents the government from forcing medical treatment without a compelling state interest that outweighs individual rights. They also contend that many public health mandates lack a rigorous legal basis, as the Supreme Court has historically ruled that pre-existing public health laws do not inherently grant the government unlimited power to compel vaccination. Beyond the courtroom, they emphasize that relying solely on coercion ignores the ethical responsibility of the state to accommodate genuine medical conflicts while avoiding a slippery slope toward broader state control over personal life.",
